what have you got in here sometimes it's  the smallest of things that hold the  most value  here we have the keys to grenfell  i just like it because like it's nice to  have like these were my keys to my house  so  you can see that like it was kind of  like burnt  sarah lost everything in the grenfell  fire but nothing compares to the loss of  her family  five of her family members died the  youngest medi was just eight years old  his everyday items are now prized  possessions  i have a bunch of marbles from mehdi's  house and you can see that they're  kind of they're not really shiny anymore  but i guess that's okay  it's impossible to imagine the trauma  this young girl has been through i don't  really like to go outside anymore  and um  yeah i just i don't know it just put me  in a little bit of a dull kind of  setting  you feel like what you're less energy  and your mood's a bit different  yeah most people think i'm being like  annoying or rude or something but it's  not that it's just  i just haven't got the energy sarah's  family are among the 72 people who died  in the tower five years ago  since then almost one and a half  thousand children have been treated for  trauma and the demand isn't going away  the doctor in charge says many children  have been left questioning their sense  of safety bonfire night can feel quite  triggering for people even you know  candles on a birthday cake happen some  people being quite triggering people  feeling you know a bit worried about you  know electrics in the home  and and feeling like they need to check  that things are switched off and just  being kind of hyper aware of safety in a  way that  most  children don't have to be but not all  children want therapy here in this  setting many many more rely on  grassroots organizations for support and  in this community they get it  at this charity they believe in the  power of play using art and activities  to help children grieve cope and begin  to heal  after the grandfather fire we went as  many children that were suffering  significant trauma but this really  ranged you know some children were  becoming very introverts and they didn't  want to talk to anybody and then some  children you could just see it and their  anger and their aggression that's how  they were expressing their trauma and  their sadness and i think that the only  way that you can compare that is to when  you're in the water and you're living  somewhere and then you just got to be  whisked away to a safe country so it was  like that so children were coming to us  and they had suffered you know beyond  trauma it was awful  for sean and his team grenfell was  something they felt personally a family  they supported died in the fire they  keep the memory of them alive in a  special legacy funding trips to  disneyland paris offering children an  escape from a reality which still haunts  them  because for hundreds of children what  happened on the 14th of june 2017 will  always be with them charlotte lomas sky  news
 

